Day 6 - Access Specifiers in C++

Today I learned about access specifiers in C++. After learning about encapsulation, I wanted to understand how C++ actually controls access to data inside a class.

I found that access specifiers help decide which variables and functions should be available to everyone and which should stay hidden.

What are Access Specifiers?

Access specifiers are keywords that control the visibility of class members.

C++ provides three access specifiers:

* public
* private
* protected

They help us protect data and control how a class is used.

Public

Members declared as public can be accessed from anywhere in the program.
  Private

Members declared as private cannot be accessed directly outside the class.

  Protected

Protected members are similar to private members, but they can be accessed by derived classes when inheritance is used.

I haven’t used protected much yet, but I learned that it becomes important while working with inheritance.

Program I Tried
  #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Student {
public:
    string name;

private:
    int age;

public:
    void setAge(int a) {
        age = a;
    }

    int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
};

int main() {

    Student s;

    s.name = "Payal";

    s.setAge(20);

    cout << "Name: " << s.name << endl;
    cout << "Age: " << s.getAge() << endl;

    return 0;
}
